# Tehcnology Stack
- Always keep backend and frontend separated between each other

## Backend
- Always write the backend code inside the backend folder, unless it is stated othewise.
- Use Ruby 3.2
- Use Ruby on Rails 7.2 for the Backend application logic.
- Configure the Ruby on Raills application to use PostgreSQL as the database
- Always use Ruby on Rails generators when creating a new endpoint, model or test
- Always use jbuilder template files when responding any json response
- Include debug gem in the development and test gemfile groups and use it as debugger.
- Include dotenv-rails gem in the development and test gemfile groups and use it to load environment variables from local .env files in the project
- Include factory_bot_rails gem in the development and test gemfile groups and use it to generate mock data in an organized way.
- Include faker gem in the development and test gemfile groups and use it as a surce of mock data for factory_bot_rails gem methods.
- Include rubocop-factory_bot, rubocop-rails and rubocop-rspec gems in the development and test gemfile groups and use them as linter and make sure to rub `rubocop` at the end of each code generartion to make sure the code is properly formatted.
- Include rspec-rails gem in the test gemfile group and use it as core test framework instead of the default rails test framework
- Include shoulda-matchewrs gem in the test gemfile group and use it to test rails models features, such as relationships and validations
- Always use the config/database.yml file to connect to the database and use Enviaroment variables to define database name, database host, database usename and database 
- Always add a comment parameter of the columns of new tables
- Always add a comment parametter when adding a new column in an existing table
- Always run rails or rake or any rails related command inside the backend container use the docker compose file define at the root of the project for referece

### Testing
- Under the context of backend code and testing such code the term "Framework Component" refers to any part that makes the Ruby on Rails framework, the different components that exists whitin the context of the frakework are:
  - Models
  - Migrations
  - Controllers
  - Views
  - Helpers
  - Rake Tasks
  - Routes
  - Mailers
- When a prompt and/or rule use the word "spec" it referes a test file for a framework component
- When a prompt and/or rule use the word "test" it referes to a specific test case of a framework component
- Always use rspec to create new specs. Under no circumstance use the default rails test framework
- Always When implementing a new feature create a spec for each of the framework component that feature requires with their corresponding test cases, In other words, if the new feature requires to create a new route, a new endpoint and a new model create a test for each one of those components validatig their functionality.
- Always check if there is an existent rspec spec file before creating a spec for any component. If the spec already exists add the new test case to that spec
- When creating test cases for any framework component create them using the `context` rspec method and group test cases into similar context in a way that the final test output looks similar to user story I.E:

```
context 'WHEN a user registers' do
  context 'AND the there are not errors' do
    it 'should respond with 201 status code do
      # TEST CODE HERE...
    end
  end

  context 'AND it did not suply an email' do
    it 'should not create any new user register' do
      # TEST CODE HERE...
    end
    it 'should respond with a 400 status code' do
      # TEST CODE HERE...
    end
    it 'should respond with a response body with a message "Missing email"' do
      # TEST CODE HERE...
    end
  end

  context 'AND Password is invalid' do
    it 'should not create any new user register' do
      # TEST CODE HERE...
    end
    it 'should respond with a 400 status code' do
      # TEST CODE HERE...
    end
    it 'should respond with a response body with a message "Passwords Invalid"' do
      # TEST CODE HERE...
    end
  end
```

## Frontend
- When writing code for the frontend the term "Componen" used in any rule and/or prompt refers to React UI Component
- Use vite to setup the proyect
- Use typescript
- Use React
- Use Chakra UI as design system and UI framework
- Use React Router to route between pages
- Use Axios to make request to the backend
- Use react-query and use it alongside axios to make request to the backend using hooks
- Use DayJS to manipulate dates
- Use React Testing Library
- Use Sass instead of css
- The general structure of the src folder is the follwing one:
